  "_about": "Marcus's house in Bretton Oaks, the one you can walk into (scripts/split_house.gd, Alex 2026-11-17: 'the living room represents half of the total size and the upper floor is like the other right half and then the basement is underground'). A back-split: from the street it is the same brick ranch as its neighbours. Inside, the main floor is the left half at ground level; a half flight up from the living room is the bedroom floor over the right half (upper_y), beside it a flight down to the basement under the bedroom half (basement_y, 'basement_footprint'), as in Alex's plans (2026-11-17): the black-tile rec room across the back and the green shag den across the front, the same size, with the bathroom beside the stairs. Every number is in the house's own space (Godot: x to the right as you face the front, z toward the street, the origin on the ground in the middle of the footprint), for an unmirrored house. 'house' is its index round the cul-de-sac (data/suburb.json). Rooms: a floor rect [x, z, width, depth] or several, a floor material (tile_beige, carpet_grey, tile_black, shag_green, cement, carpet_blue, carpet_cream, tile_white) and a wall colour. Walls: [x0, z0, x1, z1] centre lines, axis-aligned, with 'doors' [[at, width, kind], ...] along them (kind: 'open' a plain gap to the ceiling, the default; 'door' a hinged door that swings as she comes up; 'slide' sliding glass doors; 'window' glass between a sill and a head that still blocks the way); the world editor's house tools draw and change them; 'from' and 'to' are their heights, and 'block': false keeps one off the walking grid (the ensuite's wall over the stairwell, with the flight running under it). Ramps: the stairs, a rect over which the floor climbs along +x from 'from' to 'to'. Furniture: catalogue ids (data/furniture.json), 'at' [x, z], 'turn' degrees, 'y' for a piece on another one, 'tint' an html colour for the piece's fabric or paint (its 'colors' parts in data/furniture.json, or only 'tint_parts'), 'over' true for a piece that stands over the stairs (the ensuite's tub, on the floor laid over the basement flight) and so mustn't block the walking grid there. 'tones': the colour each named material of the furniture takes over its own grain (greyed first, the pattern kept), so the house's oak isn't orange. Every name is a draft until Alex approves it. Outside doors ('outside_doors', Alex 2026-11-17: a sliding door out the back of the dining room): the shell's wall on that 'side' is cut open at 'at' (x), 'width' wide, for this house alone, and the door hangs at 'z'.",
